course: chinese-street
lesson: 10
title: Grandparents, aunties and uncles — how families address elders
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When Ming's grandad comes in, Ming's mum whispers «kuài jiào yéye!» (quick, say hello to Grandad!). In Chinese families you greet each elder by their title, out loud: «yéye hǎo!». A silent child looks rude, even if he is only shy.
explain: There are separate words for each side of the family. Dad's parents are yéye and nǎinai; in the north, mum's parents are lǎoye and lǎolao (in the south, wàigōng and wàipó). Dad's sister is gūgu, mum's brother jiùjiu, mum's sister yímā, dad's older brother bóbo.
explain: The titles stretch outside the family: any man about your dad's age is shūshu (uncle), any woman about your mum's age is āyí (auntie), any old man in the park yéye. You never call an adult by his first name.
explain: At New Year all the qīnqi (relatives) come, including people nobody can quite explain, and Ming's mum whispers the right title to say.
fact: yéye («YEH?-yeh») is grandad on dad's side; children also call any old man yéye.
fact: nǎinai («NIGH-nigh», the first dipping) is granny on dad's side, and any old lady to a child; children never call her by her name.
fact: lǎoye («LAOW-yeh») is grandad on mum's side in the north; in the south he is wàigōng, where wài means outside, from an old custom families today do not mean unkindly.
fact: lǎolao («LAOW-laow») is granny on mum's side in the north (wàipó in the south); mixing her up with nǎinai makes everyone laugh.
fact: shūshu («SHOO-shoo») is uncle and any man around dad's age; Lǐ shūshu is how a child says Mr Li.
fact: āyí («AH-ee?») is auntie, any woman around mum's age, and also a polite word for a nanny or cleaner; Wáng āyí is how a child says Mrs Wang.
fact: bóbo («BAW?-baw») is dad's older brother or an older man; shūshu is younger than dad or about the same age.
fact: gūgu («GOO-goo») is dad's sister; it is the proper word, not baby talk.
fact: jiùjiu («JYO-jyo») is mum's brother, in many families a special, spoiling uncle.
fact: yímā («EE?-mah») is mum's sister; aunties are split by side: gūgu on dad's side, yímā on mum's.
fact: kuài jiào yéye («KWIGH JYOW YEH?-yeh») means quick, greet Grandad; jiào rén means greeting people by their titles, and the answer is yéye hǎo!
fact: qīnqi («CHIN-chee») means relatives, the wider family, as in qīnqi dōu lái le (all the relatives have come).
exercise: Who is yéye? | Grandad on dad's side | Grandad on mum's side | Dad's brother | Any young man
exercise: Who is nǎinai? | Granny on dad's side | Granny on mum's side | Mum's sister | A baby girl
exercise: In the north, what do kids call mum's dad? | lǎoye | yéye | bóbo | shūshu
exercise: In the north, mum's mum is | lǎolao | nǎinai | gūgu | āyí
exercise: In the south, mum's parents are called | wàigōng and wàipó | yéye and nǎinai | shūshu and āyí | lǎoye and lǎolao
exercise: What does a child call a man around dad's age, like Ming's dad? | shūshu | gēge | yéye | lǎobà
exercise: How does a child say Mrs Wang? | Wáng āyí | āyí Wáng | Xiǎo Wáng | Wáng nǎinai
exercise: Who is bóbo? | Dad's older brother, or an older man | Dad's younger brother, the youngest uncle | Mum's brother | A baby boy in the family
exercise: gūgu is | Dad's sister | Mum's sister | Dad's mum | A baby word for dog
exercise: Who is jiùjiu? | Mum's brother | Dad's brother | Grandad | Cousin
exercise: yímā is | Mum's sister | Dad's sister | Mum's mum | Big sister
exercise: «kuài jiào yéye!» means | Quick, say hello to Grandad! | Quick, call Grandad on the phone! | Quick, Grandad is coming, hide! | Quick, run to Grandad's!
exercise: What is the right answer to «kuài jiào yéye»? | yéye hǎo! | bāibāi! | wǒ jiào Sam! | méi guānxi!
exercise: What are qīnqi? | Relatives, the wider family | Neighbours in the same block of flats | Classmates | Shopkeepers on your street
exercise: Why does a silent child look rude when an elder arrives? | Greeting each elder by title is the polite thing | Children are expected to sing a little song for elders | Elders need to hear your full name | Silence means you're angry with them
exercise: Can a child call an adult by his first name? | No, a child uses a title like shūshu | Yes, always, it's the friendly way to do it | Only at school, with the teachers | Only if he's tall
exercise: What does wài in wàigōng mean? | Outside | Old | Big | Kind
test: «ming's nan on his mum's side, I called her nǎinai, everyone laughed» Why? | She is lǎolao (or wàipó); nǎinai is dad's side | nǎinai is a rude word for old ladies | nǎinai means grandad, not granny | You should call her by her own first name, like a friend
test: You walk past Ming's grandad without a word because you're shy. How might it look? | A bit rude; say yéye hǎo | Very polite and quiet | Normal, nobody greets elders | Like you know him well
test: «just call his dad Jason, that's his name innit» Which is right? | A child calls him shūshu, never the first name | Yes, first names are polite and friendly | Call him lǎobà, like Ming does | Call him Xiǎo Jason
test: Ming's mum's brother always brings sweets. What is he? | jiùjiu | bóbo | gūgu | yéye
test: In a cartoon a kid calls the old man in the park «gēge». What fits better? | yéye | dìdi | bàba | lǎobà
test: «gūgu is baby talk lol» Which is right? | No, it's the proper word for dad's sister | Yes, it's baby talk for a little doggy, innit | Yes, it's what babies say for granny | No, it means grandad on mum's side
test: What would happen if you mixed up bóbo and shūshu for Ming's two uncles? | You'd mix up who is older than dad, and families care | Nothing, they are the same word said two ways | You'd call one of them auntie | You'd call one of them grandad by mistake
test: A nanny looks after Ming's little sister. What does Ming call her? | āyí | nǎinai | jiějie | lǎoshī
test: «there's like 30 people at new year and I don't know half of them» What are they? | qīnqi, relatives; Ming's mum whispers their titles | All neighbours from the block, invited for food | All teachers | Strangers from the street who came in for dumplings
test: Ming's mum's sister arrives from Manchester. What does Ming call her? | yímā | gūgu | bóbo | jiùjiu
test: «why's it wàigōng, 'outside' grandad, that's mean» What is true? | It comes from an old custom; nobody means it unkindly | It is meant as an insult to mum's side | He lives outside the house, in the garden | He is not real family, only a good friend of mum's
exam: Identify the term by which a child customarily addresses the father of his father. | yéye | lǎoye | jiùjiu | shūshu
exam: Ascertain the principle according to which terms for aunts and uncles are distinguished in Mandarin. | By the side of the family and relative age | They are distinguished solely by height and build | No such distinction exists in the language | By occupation
exam: Select the response consistent with the established usage of the expression kuài jiào yéye. | It instructs the child to greet the grandfather by his title | It is an instruction to telephone the grandfather at once | It announces the departure of the grandfather | It is a reproach addressed to the grandfather by the parents
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the use of shūshu and āyí by children? | They are respectful titles for unrelated adults of the parents' generation | They are restricted to blood relatives of the father | They are informal nicknames for elder siblings | They are reserved for teachers alone
